WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.192
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.390
SPEAKER: OS Login is a
Compute Engine offering

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.540
that provides a way to
manage SSH access to Linux

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.960
virtual machine instances
using IAM roles.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.600
When OS Login is enabled,
a Linux user account

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.600
is directly tied to a
user's Google identity.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.990
This means that a user has
a specific username and UID

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.220
in all OS Login VMs and their
project or organization.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.540
With OS Login, you can
also restrict root access.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.240
You can enable OS Login
for individual VMs

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.310
or for all VMs in your project.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.590
To enable OS Login
for a specific VM,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.930
go to the Compute
Engine VM Instances page

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.820
and select your instance.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.000
Start editing the instance and
navigate to the Custom Metadata

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:45.990
section.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.570
You want to add the
key "enable-oslogin"

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.110
and set the value to True.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.650
Then click Save.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.930
OS Login is now enabled
on this instance.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.240
And only users with the
appropriate IAM roles

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.450
can connect to the VM using SSH.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.870
Alternatively, you can enable
OS Login for all the instances

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.050
in your project by navigating
to the Compute Engine Metadata

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:11.940
page and adding "enable-oslogin"
with the value True.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.580
Once this metadata
key is added, OS Login

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.310
is enabled on all Linux
VMs in the project.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.580
You can disable OS Login on
a specific instance by adding

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.775
the metadata "enable-oslogin"
to false at the instance level.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.440
You can also enable
OS Login when

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.050
creating a VM in the
advanced security options.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.870
We will go over how to do
this in a later example.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.820
To grant a user
permission to connect

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.950
to VMs that have
OS Login enabled,

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.640
the user must have the
required OS Login IAM roles.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.370
Let's take a closer
look at these roles.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:49.550
Compute OS Admin
Login allows the user

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.360
to SSH into the Instance and Run
Commands as an administrator,

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.390
while Compute OS Login
allows them to SSH

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.820
without administrative access.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.980
The IAM service
account user role

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.140
allows a user to run operations
as a service account.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.080
This is a necessary
permission to log in

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.990
to a VM that is associated
with the service account.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.390
You can grant the
Compute OS Login roles

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.130
at the instance level
or at the project level

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.120
so they apply to all
resources inside your project.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.360
The service account user role
can be granted on the project

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.490
or on individual
service accounts.

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:25.400
In scenarios where users
outside your organization

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.880
require access to your VMs,
your organization administrator

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.000
must grant the Compute OS
Login external user role

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.930
to those users at the
organization level.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.730
Let's run through an example.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.760
We will start by creating
a Compute Engine instance.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.570
We are going to leave all
the fields as defaults.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.240
Scroll down and expand
to the Security section.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.310
Here you can see
the Manage Access

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:53.570
section and the option
Control VM Access

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.490
through IAM Permissions.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.310
This will ensure that
our newly created VM

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.830
will have OS Login enabled.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.590
Let's return to SSH
into the VM as a user

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.330
without the Compute Admin role.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.520
And it's unsuccessful.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.500
This is expected
because we have not yet

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.710
given the necessary permissions
to this user account.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.250
Let's go ahead and grant
the appropriate IAM roles

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.930
to my user account.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:24.760
In this scenario, we
would like this user

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.180
to be able to SSH into the
VM but not on any commands

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.870
as the root account.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.310
Therefore, we need to grant this
user the Compute OS Login role.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.920
Now let's try to SSH once again.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.360
Wait a few seconds.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.750
And we are able to SSH
into the VM successfully.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:50.425
In summary, OS Login can
simplify SSH access management

00:03:50.425 --> 00:03:54.070
to your Compute Engine
instances by using IAM roles.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.930
When granting
these roles, always

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.720
keep in mind the security best
practice of least privilege.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.180
And set your
permissions accordingly.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

